        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The possible remainders are $0,1,4$.\n\nWrite the integer as $4k+r$, where $r=0,1,2,3$. Its square is congruent to $r^2$ modulo 8.",
          "worked": "Write any integer as $4k+r$, where $k\\in\\mathbb Z$ and $r\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}$.\n$$\\begin{align*}(4k+r)^2&=16k^2+8kr+r^2\\\\(4k+r)^2&\\equiv r^2\\pmod 8.\\end{align*}$$\nThe four cases give\n$$\\begin{align*}0^2&\\equiv0\\pmod8,\\\\1^2&\\equiv1\\pmod8,\\\\2^2&\\equiv4\\pmod8,\\\\3^2&\\equiv1\\pmod8.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, the square of every integer leaves a remainder of 0, 1 or 4 when divided by 8.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 65
      },
      {
        "id": "p7-q19-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p7-q19-b-stem",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Suppose ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "n",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " is a natural number. Prove that ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "9^n+25^n",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " is never a perfect square.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p7-q19-b-hint",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Hint: Use part (a).",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Never a perfect square.\n\n$9^n+25^n\\equiv2\\pmod8$, whereas squares have remainder 0, 1 or 4.",
          "worked": "Since $9\\equiv1\\pmod8$ and $25\\equiv1\\pmod8$, the exponentiation and addition rules give\n$$\\begin{align*}9^n+25^n&\\equiv1^n+1^n\\pmod8\\\\9^n+25^n&\\equiv2\\pmod8.\\end{align*}$$\nBy part (a), no perfect square leaves a remainder of 2 when divided by 8. Therefore, $9^n+25^n$ is never a perfect square.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      }
    ]
  },
